feat(ui): rescue the workspace grid and give telemetry its own type

## THE COLLAPSE

.app__main declares exactly two grid tracks, minmax(0, 2fr) and
minmax(0, 1fr). It was handed THREE direct children: the record
panel, the dashboard, and the live column. The third was auto-placed
into an implicit THIRD ROW, so the whole primary column collapsed to
the height of its tallest child.

**A grid does not complain about this.** The layout rendered. Nothing
threw, no attribute was malformed, tsc was silent, and all 413 tests
passed while the page was visibly broken. The browser resolved a
well-formed request into a layout nobody wanted, which is why this
commit carries a guard rather than only a fix.

.quiet had the same shape of defect for a different reason. It was a
<div> holding <dt>/<dd> INSIDE the readout's <dl>, so the key/value
grid auto-placed a bare <div> into its max-content label track and
squeezed two paragraphs of explanation into the width of the word
"throughput". It is prose about the figures above it, not a figure.

## THE FIX IS STRUCTURAL, NOT COSMETIC

A :nth-child(3) rule would have hidden the symptom and left the cause:
the next panel dropped in would land in a track nobody chose. So
.app__primary makes the child count two BY CONSTRUCTION -- the
workspace stack is one grid item however many panels it holds -- and
.quiet moves below the grid at full width as prose.

.quiet also had to be given an explicit single-column template. It had
inherited max-content/minmax(0,1fr) from the <dl>, so fixing the
markup alone would have left the prose squeezed into a label-width
track. One explicit 1fr, rather than no declaration, so nothing above
it can silently re-narrow it.

## THE GUARD

frontend/src/app-grid-layout.test.tsx checks BOTH halves of the
invariant, because the layout is correct only when the track count and
the child count are the SAME NUMBER, and neither half is visible from
the other: two children under a three-track grid is an empty column,
three children under a two-track grid is the collapse.

The fixture supplies a real ReconciliationDiff on purpose. With
reconciliation={null} the record panel renders nothing, .app__main has
two children anyway, and the count assertion PASSED against the
reintroduced defect. It passed for the wrong reason, which is worse
than no guard. The panel only appears when there IS a difference, so a
diff is supplied -- the third child is now genuinely there to be
counted, which is the only state in which the collapse can occur.

quiet-feed.test.tsx asserted "why the rate is zero" in lower case,
which was correct when the phrase was a <dt> (this panel's labels are
all lower case) and stopped being correct when it became an <h4>.
Case is presentation and the stylesheet owns presentation, so the five
sites now compare through one shared constant. The assertions are not
weakened: each still proves presence where it belongs and absence
where it does not.

## THE DESIGN CONSTRAINTS

Queried from the ui-ux-pro-max skill, colours.csv / typography.csv /
ux-guidelines.csv, Product Type "Developer Tool / IDE".

* WEBFONTS DECLINED, THE RULE KEPT. The skill's "Dashboard Data"
  pairing is Fira Code for figures and Fira Sans for labels, and the
  transferable part is the division of labour: code for data, sans for
  labels. This console may run on a locked-down host with no egress,
  and a webfont that silently fails to load reflows every column at
  once -- the worst possible failure for a density-critical panel. Both
  roles are satisfied by fonts the OS already has.

* TWO BORDER WEIGHTS, BECAUSE THEY DO DIFFERENT JOBS. The skill
  separates a DIVIDER, which only has to be perceptible, from a
  CONTROL BOUNDARY, which is the only thing telling a keyboard user
  where the input box is and must clear 3:1. --line stays at 1.44:1
  for dividers; --line-strong at 3.46:1 is for the four form fields.
  One weight cannot be both -- it would make the dividers too loud or
  the fields invisible.

* INK ON THE ACCENT IS DARK. The accent is a light blue, so a white
  label on it measures 2.46:1 and fails. --on-accent is 7.63:1. A
  button is the one place a stylesheet is tempted to reach for white
  by reflex.

* TELEMETRY GETS THREE DECLARATIONS TOGETHER: --mono,
  tabular-nums, and "zero" for the slashed zero. Dropping any one
  produces a panel that looks nearly right, which is the failure mode
  worth guarding. Prose inside a figure slot is re-asserted to the sans,
  or "no reading yet -- one observation cannot produce a rate" would be
  set in monospace.

Every value was measured, not eyeballed: 15.93:1 ink, 7.30:1 muted,
9.08:1 focus ring, 3.14:1 marker, 6.94:1 selected border. The focus
ring is 2px at 3:1 per the skill's Focus Appearance rule, whose named
anti-pattern is a thin low-contrast outline.

## SEVEN DECLARATIONS THAT WERE BEING DROPPED

--text and --bg were renamed to --ink and --ground at some point and
four and three references respectively were never followed. An
undefined var() makes a declaration invalid at computed-value time, so
those properties were silently discarded -- including the text colour
on the wordmark, the live strip's healthy state, and both accent
buttons. Found while auditing the palette; fixed here because the same
audit would otherwise re-report them.

## HONEST SCOPE

The skill's 44x44px touch target is NOT met by the agent-list pills
(~28px). That is a deliberate trade for density on a desktop
operator's console, and it is the one constraint knowingly left
unmet. The tokens are now fully defined and fully used: zero
undefined, zero unused.
